From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:31:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fix intrinsic heights and relative insets of inline-level
 boxes

taffy resolves min-content, max-content and stretch on a node's
children, and every algorithm reading its own size style treats the
keyword as auto. In an inline formatting context blitz is that parent.
inline_box_inputs already resolves the width keywords at both
measurement sites; the height keywords were left as auto.

Resolve them in inline_box_inputs too: min-content and max-content
measure the box against that space, and stretch fills the definite space
left after the margins. Every measurement of the box goes through it, so
both agree.

A position: relative float inside an inline formatting context was
placed where the float algorithm put it, ignoring its insets. Resolve
them against the containing block (the inline container's content box,
direction-aware for left/right) and shift the float's location by them,
as relative atomic inlines already are. The out-of-flow candidates
translated with the float move with it.

Relative top and bottom percentages on an atomic inline resolved against
the inline container's final content height, which this pass is still
computing. Resolve them against the container's known or styled height
instead, and against zero when it has none, the same containing block
relative floats use.
---
 src/layout/inline.rs | 100 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 91 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/layout/inline.rs b/src/layout/inline.rs
index 67fbbba..77c99c7 100644
--- a/src/layout/inline.rs
+++ b/src/layout/inline.rs
@@ -38,8 +38,15 @@ fn subtract_margins(mut child_inputs: LayoutInput, margin: taffy::Rect<f32>) ->
 /// Layout inputs for an atomic inline box, with any sizing keyword on its `width` style
 /// (`min-content`, `max-content`, `fit-content`, `fit-content(...)`, `stretch`) resolved
 /// into the available space or known width the box is measured with.
+///
+/// `height` keywords resolve here too. taffy only resolves a sizing
+/// keyword on a node's children, and in an inline formatting context blitz is
+/// that parent: `min-content`/`max-content` measure the box against that space,
+/// and `stretch` fills the definite space left after the margins. Every
+/// measurement of the box goes through this, so both agree.
 fn inline_box_inputs(
     width_style: taffy::Dimension,
+    height_style: taffy::Dimension,
     margin: taffy::Rect<f32>,
     child_inputs: LayoutInput,
 ) -> LayoutInput {
@@ -78,6 +85,22 @@ fn inline_box_inputs(
         }
         _ => {}
     }
+
+    match height_style.tag() {
+        CompactLength::MIN_CONTENT_TAG => {
+            inputs.available_space.height = AvailableSpace::MinContent
+        }
+        CompactLength::MAX_CONTENT_TAG => {
+            inputs.available_space.height = AvailableSpace::MaxContent
+        }
+        CompactLength::STRETCH_TAG => {
+            if let Some(height) = inputs.available_space.height.into_option() {
+                inputs.known_dimensions.height = Some(height);
+                inputs.available_space.height = AvailableSpace::Definite(height);
+            }
+        }
+        _ => {}
+    }
     inputs
 }

